=== Vowels ===

Maroniko has a seven-vowel system with nasal contrast inherited from Imo:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! !! Front !! Central !! Back
|-
! Close || i ĩ || || u ũ
|-
! Close-mid || e ẽ || || o õ
|-
! Open-mid || ɛ ɛ̃ || || ɔ ɔ̃
|-
! Open || || a ã ||
|}

Nasal vowels occur primarily in Imo-derived terms and in some Kimbundu-derived vocabulary.

=== Syllable structure ===

Maroniko permits relatively complex syllable structure compared to many creoles:

* CV, CVC, CVn (where n = nasal vowel)
* Word-initial consonant clusters from Imfezzan loans: ''adrár'' (mountain), ''agelmim'' (lake)
* Word-final consonants preserved from Volisanian-influenced Sabir: ''kanch'' (courtyard), ''amich'' (friend)

=== Stress ===

Stress in Maroniko typically falls on the penultimate syllable:
* {{lang|bic|KA-sa}} — "house"
* {{lang|bic|ma-RÒN-iko}} — "Maroon speech"

Stress shifts to the ultimate syllable in words with final consonant clusters:
* {{lang|bic|a-MÍCH}} — "friend"

Irregular stress is marked with an acute accent:
* {{lang|bic|ká-fe}} — "coffee"

== Grammar ==

Maroniko grammar exhibits typical creole features while preserving certain substrate influences from its source languages.

=== Morphological typology ===

Maroniko is predominantly [[analytic language|analytic]], with minimal inflectional morphology. Nouns are not marked for case, and verbal categories are expressed through pre-verbal particles rather than conjugation.

=== Nouns and noun phrases ===

==== Number ====

Plural is optionally marked with the suffix {{lang|bic|-nan}}:
* {{lang|bic|munu}} — "person" or "people"
* {{lang|bic|munu-nan}} — "people" (emphatic plural)

==== Definiteness ====

Maroniko does not mark definiteness through articles. The distinction between definite and indefinite reference is conveyed through context or through demonstratives:
* {{lang|bic|ile}} — "house" or "a house" or "the house"
* {{lang|bic|ile wa}} — "this house"
* {{lang|bic|ile ya}} — "that house"

==== Gender ====

Maroniko has no grammatical gender. Natural gender in animate nouns may be distinguished through derived forms:
* {{lang|bic|omí}} — "man"
* {{lang|bic|omí-ya}} — "woman" (from ''omí'' + Imo-derived feminine suffix -''ya'')

=== Pronouns ===

Maroniko pronouns derive primarily from Imo:

{| class="wikitable"
! Person !! Subject !! Object !! Possessive
|-
| 1st singular || {{lang|bic|èmi}} || {{lang|bic|èmi}} || {{lang|bic|èmi}}
|-
| 2nd singular || {{lang|bic|ìwọ}} || {{lang|bic|ìwọ}} || {{lang|bic|rẹ}}
|-
| 3rd singular || {{lang|bic|óun}} || {{lang|bic|óun}} || {{lang|bic|rẹ}}
|-
| 1st plural || {{lang|bic|àwa}} || {{lang|bic|àwa}} || {{lang|bic|àwa}}
|-
| 2nd plural || {{lang|bic|ẹ̀yín}} || {{lang|bic|ẹ̀yín}} || {{lang|bic|yín}}
|-
| 3rd plural || {{lang|bic|àwọn}} || {{lang|bic|àwọn}} || {{lang|bic|wọn}}
|}

Note: Orthographic marks (grave/acute) indicate vowel quality distinctions from Imo substrate, not stress.

=== Tense-Mood-Aspect system ===

The TMA system is central to Maroniko grammar. Tense, mood, and aspect are marked by pre-verbal particles:

{| class="wikitable"
! Particle !! Function !! Example !! Translation
|-
| (unmarked) || Present/habitual || {{lang|bic|Èmi sòrò.}} || "I speak." / "I am speaking."
|-
| {{lang|bic|yá}} || Past/completive || {{lang|bic|Èmi yá sòrò.}} || "I spoke." / "I have spoken."
|-
| {{lang|bic|ló}} || Future || {{lang|bic|Èmi ló sòrò.}} || "I will speak."
|-
| {{lang|bic|stá}} || Progressive || {{lang|bic|Èmi stát sòrò.}} || "I am speaking."
|-
| {{lang|bic|tábi}} || Habitual past || {{lang|bic|Èmi tábi sòrò.}} || "I used to speak."
|-
| {{lang|bic|kábá}} || Completive ("just") || {{lang|bic|Èmi kábá sòrò.}} || "I have just spoken."
|-
| {{lang|bic|kán}} || Irrealis/subjunctive || {{lang|bic|Èmi kán sòrò.}} || "I would speak." / "I might speak."
|}

TMA particles can be combined:
* {{lang|bic|Èmi yá stát sòrò.}} — "I was speaking." (past progressive)
* {{lang|bic|Èmi ló kábá sòrò.}} — "I will have just spoken." (future perfect)

The progressive marker {{lang|bic|stát}} derives from Sabir/Volisanian ''estar''. The completive {{lang|bic|kábá}} derives from Kimbundu ''kaba'' (to finish, to cut).

=== Verbs ===

Maroniko verbs have a single invariant form used for all persons:
* {{lang|bic|èmi sòrò, ìwọ sòrò, óun sòrò, àwa sòrò}} — "I speak, you speak, s/he speaks, we speak"

==== Copula ====

Maroniko has two copula verbs:

* {{lang|bic|dá}} — Essential/identity (from Yoruba ''jẹ́'' "to be")
*: {{lang|bic|Èmi dá omí.}} — "I am a person."
* {{lang|bic|stá}} — State/location (from Sabir/Volisanian *estar*)
*: {{lang|bic|Èmi stá na ile.}} — "I am in the house."

=== Word order ===

Basic word order is SVO:
* {{lang|bic|Èmi kumi omi.}} — "I drink water." (literally "I eat water")

Noun phrase order is Determiner-Noun-Adjective:
* {{lang|bic|ile nla}} — "the big house" (literally "house big")

=== Negation ===

Negation is formed with {{lang|bic|kà}} before the verb:
* {{lang|bic|Èmi kà kumi.}} — "I don't eat."
* {{lang|bic|Èmi kà yá kumi.}} — "I didn't eat."

Negative concord is found in some constructions:
* {{lang|bic|Nlé kà kó nkan.}} — "There isn't anything there." (literally "There not be not nothing")

=== Prepositions ===

Key prepositions in Maroniko include:

* {{lang|bic|na}} — locative (from Imo ''ní'' "at/in")
*: {{lang|bic|Èmi stá na ile.}} — "I am in the house."
* {{lang|bic|di}} — genitive/possessive (from Sabir ''de'')
*: {{lang|bic|ile di óun}} — "his/her house"
* {{lang|bic|pa}} — instrumental ("with," from Sartan ''por'' via Sabir)
*: {{lang|bic|kubi pa máno}} — "hit with hand"
* {{lang|bic|pa-kúma}} — purposive ("for," from Sabir ''para'' + ''kuma'' "reason")
*: {{lang|bic|ló na pa-kúma sòrò.}} — "go for speaking"

